[SYSDESIGN]
--- SECTION: Requirements
[SAY]Alright, so for WhatsApp let me start with the functional requirements — the core things we need to build.

First, users need to send and receive messages in real time with low latency — under 500ms delivery. Second, we need to guarantee message delivery — if someone sends a message, it has to reach the recipient even if they're offline right now. Third, we need group chats supporting up to 100 participants. And fourth, users should be able to send media — images, videos, documents.

For non-functional requirements — we're targeting billions of users with high throughput. Messages should be stored on our servers only as long as necessary for delivery, not permanently. And the system needs to handle users with multiple devices.

Things I'm putting below the line for now: end-to-end encryption details, spam prevention, and read receipts.[/SAY]
[DETAIL]**Functional Requirements (Core):**
1. Users can send/receive text messages with low latency (< 500ms)
2. Guaranteed message delivery — messages reach recipients even if offline (up to 30 days)
3. Group chats with up to 100 participants
4. Users can send/receive media (images, videos, documents)

**Non-Functional Requirements:**
- Scale: 2B+ registered users, ~200M concurrent connections
- High throughput: billions of messages per day
- Minimal server-side message retention — deliver and discard
- Multi-device support per user

**Below the Line (Out of Scope):**
- End-to-end encryption implementation details
- Spam and scraping prevention
- Read receipts and typing indicators[/DETAIL]
--- SECTION: Core Entities
[SAY]Let me define the core entities. We've got four.

A User represents someone registered in the system. A Chat is a conversation — could be between two people or a group of up to 100. A Message belongs to a chat and contains the text or media reference, the sender, and a timestamp. And a Client represents a specific device — important because a single user might have a phone, tablet, and desktop all connected simultaneously.[/SAY]
[DETAIL]- Users
- Chats (2-100 users)
- Messages
- Clients (a user might have multiple devices)[/DETAIL]
--- SECTION: API Design
[SAY]Now for the API — and this is where WhatsApp differs from most systems. We're not using REST here. Chat apps have high-frequency bidirectional updates, so WebSockets are the right choice. Users open the app, establish a WebSocket connection over TLS, and all commands flow through that socket.

The key commands are: createChat to start a new conversation, sendMessage to send a message on a chat, createAttachment for media uploads, and modifyChatParticipants to add or remove users from a group.

The important pattern is acknowledgments. When a client receives a message, it sends an ack back to the server. This is how we confirm delivery actually happened — if we don't get an ack, we know we need to retry or store the message for later delivery.

On the server-to-client side, we have parallel commands: chatUpdate when a chat is created or modified, and newMessage when a message arrives. Each expects a RECEIVED acknowledgment.[/SAY]
[DETAIL]Next, we'll want to think through the API of our system. Unlike a lot of other products where a REST API is probably appropriate, for a chat app, we're going to have high-frequency updates being both sent and received. This is a perfect use case for a bi-directional socket connection !
For this interview, we'll use WebSockets (over TLS for security), although a custom protocol over a raw TLS-encrypted TCP connection would also work. The idea will be that users will open the app and connect to the server, opening this socket which will be used to send and receive commands which represent our API.
As we define our API, we'll specify the commands that are sent and received over the connection by the client.
First, let's be able to create a chat.
Now we should be able to send messages on the chat.
We need a way to create attachments (note: I'm going to amend this later in the writeup).
And we need a way to add/remove users to the chat.
Each of these commands will have parallel commands that are sent to other clients. When the command has been received by clients, they'll send an `ack` command back to the server letting it know the command has been received (and it doesn't have to be sent again)!
The message receipt acknowledgement is a bit non-obvious but crucial to making sure we don't lose messages. By forcing clients to ack, we can know for certain that the message has been delivered all the way to the client.
When a chat is created or updated ...
When a message is received ...
Etc ...
Note that enumerating all of these APIs can take time! In the actual interview, I might shortcut by only writing the command names and not the full API. It's also usually a good idea to summarize the API initially before you build out the high-level design in case things need to change. "I'll come back to this as I learn more" is completely acceptable!
Our whiteboard might look like this:
Commands Exchanged
Now that we have a base to work with let's figure out how we can implement them while we satisfy our requirements.

